.TH WAKEFUL 1 "2026" "wakeful" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wakeful \- keep a KDE Plasma session awake for a set time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wakeful
[\fB\-\-no\-tray\fR] [\fB\-v\fR]
.br
.B wakeful start
[\fB\-\-screen\fR] [\fIDURATION\fR]
.br
.B wakeful extend
\fIDURATION\fR
.br
.B wakeful stop
.br
.B wakeful status
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no command,
.B wakeful
puts an icon in the system tray. From its menu a block can be started for a
preset length, a custom length, or until it is turned off, then extended or
turned off. Left\-clicking the icon starts a block of the last length used, or
turns a running one off. The tooltip shows the time remaining.
.PP
A block is released when its time runs out, when it is turned off, and when
.B wakeful
exits or crashes: the inhibitions are held on its own D\-Bus connection, and
the session's power management drops them when that connection closes.
.PP
With a command,
.B wakeful
asks the running tray to act, which makes it usable from scripts and from a
custom keyboard shortcut.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B Keep awake, screen can turn off
The default. Idle sleep is blocked; the screen still dims, turns off and locks
on its usual timers. Uses org.freedesktop.PowerManagement.Inhibit.
.TP
.B Keep awake and screen on
Also blocks dimming, turning the screen off and idle screen locking. Adds
org.freedesktop.ScreenSaver.Inhibit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBstart\fR [\fB\-\-screen\fR] [\fIDURATION\fR]
Start a block, replacing any running one. Without a duration it lasts until
stopped. \fB\-\-screen\fR keeps the screen on too.
.TP
\fBextend\fR \fIDURATION\fR
Add time to the running block.
.TP
.B stop
End the running block.
.TP
.B status
Print what is being kept awake and for how long, and which Claude Code
sessions are working.
.PP
\fIDURATION\fR is minutes as a bare number (\fB90\fR), or hours, minutes and
seconds such as \fB1h30m\fR, \fB45m\fR or \fB20s\fR. \fBforever\fR means no end.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tray
Run without a tray icon, controlled only through the commands above.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log debug detail to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CLOSING THE LID
Closing the lid is an explicit request to sleep, and no inhibition stops it.
The tray's \fBAlso ignore closing the lid\fR option instead sets the lid action
in \fI~/.config/powerdevilrc\fR to "Do nothing" for every power profile while a
block runs, and puts the previous values back when it ends. The previous values
are written to \fI$XDG_STATE_HOME/wakeful/lid\-restore.json\fR before anything
is changed. If
.B wakeful
is killed, a watchdog process restores them; if the watchdog is also gone (for
example after a reboot), the next start of
.B wakeful
restores them. A value changed in System Settings during the block is left as
it is.
.SH CLAUDE CODE
With the tray's \fBKeep awake while Claude is working\fR option on, idle sleep
is blocked while any Claude Code session of the same user on this computer is
running a turn or still has a shell running. Sessions at their prompt, or
waiting for an answer, do not count. The working sessions are listed in the
tray menu and by \fBwakeful status\fR. They are read from the files Claude Code
keeps in \fI$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/sessions\fR (default \fI~/.claude/sessions\fR),
every five seconds; a file whose process has gone is ignored. The option is
offered only when that configuration directory exists.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/wakeful/wakeful.conf
Preferences: mode, last length, lid option, Claude Code option, notifications.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/wakeful.desktop
Present when "Start at login" is on.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/wakeful/lid\-restore.json
The lid values to restore; present only while the lid is being ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 when the tray is not running or refused the command. 2 for a
duration that cannot be parsed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd\-inhibit (1)
.PP
https://github.com/stuckj/wakeful
